 (:adr/id "adr-2609092000-a-facilitator-that-teaches-what-it-refuses")
 (:adr/doc_type "adr")
 (:adr/topic "a-facilitator-that-teaches-what-it-refuses")
 (:adr/title "x402.nexus: a request that cannot be evaluated is not a verdict — typed errors, seller self-service, webhooks, and a docs site that names the libraries")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-09")
 (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
 (:adr/repos (vector "network-awai/nexus-x402" "kotoba-lang/x402-directory" "kotoba-lang/x402-sdk" "com-junkawasaki/root"))
 (:adr/body "## What this session changed, and what it measured first

Three landings on x402.nexus, each starting from a measurement against the
LIVE service rather than from a reading of the code.

### 1. A request that could not be evaluated stopped answering as a verdict

Measured live: `POST /verify {\"nonsense\":1}` answered **200** with
`{\"isValid\":false,\"invalidReason\":\"unsettled: tx-not-found\"}`. Nothing about
that request had been evaluated -- `payment` and `requirements` were both
absent, `pay.x402/payload-errors` compared nil to nil, found every field equal,
and the chain was asked for the receipt of transaction `undefined`. The caller
was told their transaction is not on chain, so the first thing every integrator
does (send a half-right body) sent them to debug their wallet.

The line now drawn: **400 = we could not evaluate this, no chain call was
made. 200 + isValid:false = we evaluated it and the payment is bad.**
Mismatches between payment and requirements stay on the 200 side -- paying on
the wrong chain is a real answer about a real payment.

Two things the old path also threw away: `verdict` throws ex-info NAMING the
network it has no RPC for and the handler replaced it with the string `bad
request`; and there was no request id anywhere. Both fixed -- every response
now carries `x-request-id`, derived from `cf-ray` so the id a caller quotes is
the id in the edge log, computed once per request and threaded so header and
body cannot disagree.

`/openapi.json` documented neither `/verify` nor `/settle` -- the two routes
that MAKE this a facilitator, neither of which takes a credential -- while the
HTML page for humans had listed them the whole time. Wrong way round for a
service whose buyers are programs.

### 2. Idempotency, MCP tools, seller keys, settlement webhooks

* `Idempotency-Key` on `/settle` only (the one route with an effect to be
  unsure about). Body fingerprinted AS RECEIVED, not re-serialised: two
  encoders ordering keys differently mean the same request. Does NOT replace
  the spend gate -- the gate refuses a second CHARGE (a replay), the key makes
  a second ATTEMPT harmless (a retry). KV has no CAS, so two simultaneous
  first attempts can both proceed; stated rather than papered over.
* `verify_payment` / `settle_payment` over MCP. Neither takes a credential
  over HTTP, so this grants an agent nothing it did not already have.
* Seller keys. The only settlement route was `/admin/settlements/<seller>`
  behind ADMIN_TOKEN -- the OPERATOR's credential, which would hand a seller
  every other seller's ledger. `/apply` already proves domain control; it now
  exchanges that, once, for `sk_live_`. Stored as sha256. Rotation is proved by
  the ORIGIN, deliberately not by the old key: the reason to rotate is usually
  that the old one is lost. `GET /v1/me` `/v1/settlements` `/v1/events`,
  `POST /v1/keys/rotate`. The seller comes from the key and never from the URL.
* Webhooks, with the promise stated in full: **one delivery attempt, no
  retries.** The event is durable BEFORE it is sent (appended, awaited) and
  delivered via `ctx.waitUntil`; `GET /v1/events?since=` is the contract and
  the webhook is a latency optimisation over polling it. Emitted from the
  gateway path too -- gateway proxy is the mode that exists so a seller does
  NOT run its own gate, and it would otherwise have been the one seller with
  no events at all.

Found on the way: `nexus.apply/origin-host` ACCEPTED IP literals while both
error messages it feeds had said `no IP literal` the whole time (`host-re`
admits 127.0.0.1). A refusal that exists only in the message it would have
printed is not a refusal -- and this host is fetched.

### 3. The docs surface became a docs site

Measured on the live page before any of it: the first line of code was ~2,900px
down under a 31-row inventory table; npm / pip / cargo / go get appeared **zero
times** in the HTML, in /llms.txt and in /skill.md while all four client
libraries had been published for eight days; there were **zero**
`prefers-color-scheme` rules; and /skill.md named three MCP tools while the
manifest served five.

`x402-directory` grew `:pre-sections-html` so the operator, not the library,
decides whether a quickstart sits above or below the inventory. Language tabs
are radio inputs and sibling selectors with no script -- partly for the usual
reasons and mostly because a model fetching the page reads the MARKUP, so all
five snippets are in the text whether or not a browser selects one.

`nexus.theme` said `DADS has no dark palette`. True when written, false since
`jp-go-dds.dark` grew one: an implementation state written down as a property
of the design system, still deciding the page's appearance after the reason
expired.

## What the checks taught, which is the part worth keeping

**Four checks in this session were satisfied by absence before they were
fixed.** That is the workspace's recurring defect, met four more times:

1. `payload-errors` answered `[]` for nil-against-nil, so the shape check
   passed and the chain reported on a transaction nobody had named.
2. The cross-seller test asserted on the envelope's `seller` field -- which
   comes from the key -- instead of on the entries. A mutation that kept the
   correct LABEL and served one seller's rows to everyone PASSED. This is the
   security boundary of the whole seller API and it was being checked by
   reading back a label.
3. The `:pre-sections-html` ordering test compared `.indexOf` values, and
   `.indexOf` answers -1 for absent, so `(< -1 n)` is true: deleting the
   emission entirely left the test green.
4. Two invented CSS token names (`--color-background`, `--color-text-body`)
   shipped into the docs stylesheet. An unmapped custom property resolves to
   NOTHING and the rule collapses in silence; the page looked unchanged.

Only mutation testing found 2 and 3; only rendering found 4.

**And one instrument lied in the other direction.** I concluded twice that dark
mode was broken because two screenshots were byte-identical. They were
identical because headless Chrome on this machine defaults to
`prefers-color-scheme: dark`, so BOTH were dark. Computed styles from the
browser settled it in one call; a pixel comparison never could have, because it
cannot tell `no difference` from `no difference measured`.

**The deterministic design-quality audit scored 100.00 before and after.** It
has converged on its ten axes and sees none of: content order, whether a
published library is mentioned, language coverage, dark support, or copy
affordances. Recorded here as a no-regression floor, explicitly NOT as evidence
of improvement -- a converged gate quoted as a score is the same defect this
ADR is otherwise about.")))
